  "Question": "How many real numbers \\(x\\) satisfy the equation \\(3^{2x+2}-3^{x+3}-3^x+3=0?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(0\\)",
    "B": "\\(1\\)",
    "C": "\\(2\\)",
    "D": "\\(3\\)",
    "E": "\\(4\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "The exponents involve both \\(x\\) and \\(2x\\), suggesting that we treat \\(3^x\\) as one quantity. Let \\(t=3^x\\), noting that \\(t>0\\) for every real \\(x\\). The power laws give\n\\[\n3^{2x+2}=9t^2,\n\\qquad\n3^{x+3}=27t.\n\\]\nThus the original equation is equivalent to\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n9t^2-28t+3&=0,\\\\\n(9t-1)(t-3)&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe only possibilities are \\(t=1/9\\) and \\(t=3\\), and both satisfy the required condition \\(t>0\\).\n\nSince \\(1/9=3^{-2}\\) and \\(3=3^1\\), these give \\(x=-2\\) and \\(x=1\\). The function \\(3^x\\) is strictly increasing, so each value of \\(t\\) corresponds to exactly one real \\(x\\). All transformations were equivalent, so these are exactly the two solutions. The answer is \\(\\boxed{2}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
